WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.170
SPEAKER: Hello,
welcome to the session

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.450
on building the right API team.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.202
As part of this
video, we'll talk you

00:00:11.202 --> 00:00:12.660
through our
recommendations when it

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.020
comes to forming your API
team, the responsibilities,

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.450
and the various roles involved.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.600
So to start things
off, let's take a look

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.650
at the digital value chain.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.630
You might have seen
this diagram before.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.150
But to briefly explain,
the digital value chain

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.480
shows the relationship
between the various teams

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.090
and technologies from
the aimed consumer

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.250
all the way down to
the back end systems.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.010
For this session, will
be focusing specifically

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.380
on the API team shown
here in the chain.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:45.180
The API team builds, yep,
you guessed it, the APIs.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.730
This is the team that will be
working directly with your API

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.140
platform to create lovable
APIs for your app developers.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.130
In terms of responsibility,
the API team owns 5 core areas.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.110
First is building
highly consumable APIs.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.330
The keyword here is consumable.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.210
We recommend approaching API
design and implementation

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.810
from the outside in,
rather than the inside out.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.420
What this means is that
the API team will always

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.680
need to keep the end consumer
in mind when approaching APIs

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.990
in the case of our
digital value chain,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.250
this is the app developer.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.680
The API team should
always be asking itself,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.380
if I were an app developer,
what type of APIs what

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.030
I want to consume?

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.780
Second is learning and applying
best practices to the APIs

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.340
that they will be building.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.680
We promote a variety of
industry best practices

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.770
when it comes to the design,
development, and the deployment

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.630
of your APIs.

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.810
Please refer to the Docs page
or a self-service training

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.300
on Kyocera for
additional details.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.520
Third is engagement.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.610
Similar to our focus
on consumable APIs,

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.310
the API team should be
engaged with all the players

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.560
in the digital value chain.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.870
This includes back
end system teams,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.840
which the APIs will front,
to the app developer,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.960
in order to gain valuable
insights to unearth areas

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.130
to further iterate on and to
enhance your existing services.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.050
Scalability is
also very important

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.870
when it comes to
your API program.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:17.670
Oftentimes, we see that the API
team starts small, but grows

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.370
rapidly as more and more
demand comes down the pipeline.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.250
In these scenarios, federation
becomes very important.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.730
The API team owns the
creation and the management

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.400
of materials that allows
for the entire organization

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.200
to rapidly scale.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.380
This includes things like the
API playbook, automated test

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.120
suites, and more.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.390
Finally, API should
always be improving.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.310
By leveraging capabilities
of the platforms,

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:46.030
such as analytics, the API team
can gain valuable insight into

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:48.490
who's using the APIs and how.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.470
This information
allows the API team

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:54.460
to find areas of improvement and
enhancements based on real data

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:55.840
that they are seeing.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.120
Remember our focus
on consumability?

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.260
This information can help
a lot with that we strongly

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.010
believe in the fast
and iterative approach

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.600
to development.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:07.270
Market shifts happen,
rapidly and your API team

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.240
needs to be able to adapt and
be flexible in its delivery

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:11.670
approach.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:14.010
As a result, we
recommend a methodology

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.560
such as agile for your program.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.070
Similarly, the API team is
composed in a similar fashion

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.520
to a standard scrum team.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.400
A diverse set of perspectives
is also very important,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.820
which we'll cover more detail
when we talk about roles.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.580
Having different viewpoints
that encompass the business,

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.650
technical, and
operational views,

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.510
will streamline your
API development process.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.090
So now that we've
covered the basics,

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:41.480
let's jump into the individual
roles of an API team.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.720
There are 5 main roles when
it comes to a good API team.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.900
They are the API team
leader, the architect,

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.390
the API developers,
the evangelists,

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.340
and the extended team.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.950
Let's spend the next few
minutes going over each one.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.860
The API team leader is
essentially a product owner.

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:01.610
Now note that I said product
owner and not project manager.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:04.490
As mentioned before, the
API team we're suggesting

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.780
is based off the
standard scrum model.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.540
So the API team
leader is essentially

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:11.150
your product owner in scrum.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.250
As such, the team
leader should be

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.740
aligned with both the
business and the IT,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.990
and have a good understanding
of the needs of the consumer,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.170
for example, the app developer.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.130
The team leader also helps
create, refine, and prioritize

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.700
the scope for the API team.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.620
When it comes down to
it, the team leader

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.470
will be the one that makes the
call if trade offs are needed

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.290
in the event that the API team
doesn't have the bandwidth

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.270
to complete all the items.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.300
The architect is
critical to the API team.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.850
Not only do they bridge the
gap between the API team

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:45.340
leader and the API
developers, they also

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.160
establish and enforce
API best practices

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.130
and keep a common
design cadence.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.140
Their approach to
the API design is

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.430
consumer focused and educates
the wider API team on how

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.710
to leverage the API Platform.

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:02.650
In summary, they're part
designer, part teacher,

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:04.620
and part enforcer.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.342
The API developers is your
core development group.

00:05:07.342 --> 00:05:09.300
At the end of the day,
these are the folks that

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.870
are going to get the job done.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.200
The API developers will
look to the API team

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.320
leader for guidance on the
scope and the architect

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.480
for their design principles.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.600
They will then take those inputs
and build out the actual APIs.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.940
As you would expect,
the API developers

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.330
should be very familiar
with the API Platform.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.280
Without the API
developers, nothing

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.280
will get built, so be
sure you're nice them.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.790
The evangelist's is the
mascot for the API program.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.520
They're responsible for not
only outreaching to your API

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.950
consumers, but also
creating and nurturing

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.060
a developer community.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.710
They can provide
valuable feedback

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:47.480
from the field, which
can then directly

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.660
impact the direction
of the API program.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.180
A good evangelist will
be critical in expanding

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.640
the audience of your
APIs, both within

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.830
and outside of
your organization.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.070
The extended team is also very
important in getting your APIs

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:02.650
out to production.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.740
The extended team covers
organizations such as security,

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.800
QA, operations, and others.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.960
The extended team
and their processes

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:14.030
may vary depending on how
your organization is set up.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:15.760
But the key takeaway
is that these teams

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.220
should support rather than
obstruct your time to market.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:22.120
So before we wrap up, let's
cover some common attributes

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.930
that all successful
API teams have.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:28.440
First, a successful API
team should have the support

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.970
of an executive sponsor.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.160
Chances are, the API
team will continue

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.990
to push the envelope
of innovation

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:36.860
within your organization.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.920
So having a strong
executive sponsor

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.050
can help clear any
of those roadblocks

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:44.520
that they may face when
challenging the status quo.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.020
Second, the API teams should
be solely focused on APIs.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.440
We often see API teams consist
of developers who are also

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.770
shared with other projects.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.900
This not only can lead to issues
with resourcing and bandwidth,

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.230
but also affect the
direction of your APIs,

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.330
such as cutting corners
and striving away

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:03.830
from best practices.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.350
Third, there must be a
willingness to train others

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.210
within the organization.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.540
As your API program
grows, so will the demand.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:14.420
This in turn can lead to
an influx of APIs that will

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.510
need to be created or enhanced.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.020
Chances are the API team that
you start off with will expand,

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:22.760
and as new members
are brought in,

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.980
they need to be trained
on the foundational design

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.020
philosophies you've
already established.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:30.020
In the world of APIs and the
consumer focused approach,

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.180
a consistent API
experience is key.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.290
Fourth, the API team should have
a direct line to the business.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.390
There shouldn't be any
silos between the business

00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:40.950
and technical teams.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.510
Makes sense, right?

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.030
If the API team has a better
understanding of the business

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.880
vision and the impacts
to the end consumers,

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.890
the better the APIs
will be, since they

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:53.320
will ensure that the business
vision is properly achieved.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.680
Finally, the API
team should be free

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:57.810
from pre-existing constraints.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.070
As we mentioned
before, the API team

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.190
should strive on
innovation and not

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:06.480
be constrained by outdated
governance models or processes.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.610
In the world of
APIs, time to market

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.950
is critical, so welcome
change rather than

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:14.100
trying to fit it within
an insufficient process.

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:17.130
In closing we totally understand
of the concept discussed

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:20.280
in this video may seem a little
challenging, especially if you

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.020
already have a
governance or delivery

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.220
structure in place today.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:27.210
Remember that the start always
requires the greatest effort,

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.130
but if you can overcome
these challenges

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.140
and embrace the ideas
we just covered,

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.840
you have a more robust
and innovative API team.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.589
Thank you.

